  ${\let \sigma \eltsigma \protect \mathelt {g}}$ act on a function by $({\let
  \sigma \eltsigma \protect \mathelt {g}}f)(\protect \mathbf {r})=f(\protect
  \mathbf {r}P({\let \sigma \eltsigma \protect \mathelt {g}}))$, that is, by
  $U({\let \sigma \eltsigma \protect \mathelt {g}})^{-1}$. We identify states
  by their wavefunctions, so for the irreps of $\protect \mathsf {G}$ our
  labels, partner indices, and coupling coefficients are the \protect
  \pgatlasfont {PG Atlas}'s for the same state. Our explicit symmetry-adapted
  states agree with its tabulated ones up to the phase of each copy. Our
  matrices are its matrices at the inverse element, $D^{\Gamma }({\let \sigma
  \eltsigma \protect \mathelt {g}})=D^{\Gamma }_{\protect \mathrm
  {atlas}}({\let \sigma \eltsigma \protect \mathelt {g}}^{-1})=D^{\Gamma
  }_{\protect \mathrm {atlas}}({\let \sigma \eltsigma \protect \mathelt
  {g}})^{\dagger }$, and for a complex-conjugate pair of one-dimensional irreps
  our character is the conjugate of the tabulated one. The molecule-frame part
  of our rotor ket $\mathinner {|{{}^{J,p}_{\protect \,m},k}\rangle }$ is the
  \protect \pgatlasfont {PG Atlas}'s $(-1)^k\protect \bigl \lvert
  {}^{J,p}_{\protect \,-k}\protect \bigr \rangle $, and $\protect \mathsf
  {O(3)}/\protect \mathsf {G}$ is its $\protect \mathsf {G}\backslash \protect
  \mathsf {O(3)}$ read on column vectors. The README of Ref.~\cite
  {DasRepo2026} states the same dictionary for the irreps of $\protect \mathsf
  {G}$.}\BibitemShut {Stop}%
